From 412ea79b81c60b8c83f4405fcdb76b442f647a97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ken5998 <46025606+Ken5998@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:42:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/28] perf(video): translate each vertex program into its own
 shader

ub_vs interprets the vertex program per vertex, keeping the temporaries,
inputs and outputs in arrays it indexes at run time. Adreno puts such
arrays in memory, not registers: on the Retroid Pocket 6 the game ran at
10-12 fps at 1280x720 with the GPU at 100%.

Each program (the slots from the start slot to the final flag) now gets
a variant written out by vs_source as straight-line HLSL, one block per
instruction with every field a constant and every register its own
variable, compiled on the pixel variants' workers. Draws keep using
ub_vs until the variant is ready, so nothing changes on screen while it
compiles. Pre-transformed draws get a variant of their own without the
interpreter. RECOMP_VS_SPEC=0 turns this off, =sync compiles on the
drawing thread (for tests).

vkd3d's HLSL compiler is slow, so a variant leaves out what it cannot
reach: the pixel shaders' source (vertex variants need nothing past
ub_vs), the other kind of draw's branch and the operands an instruction
does not read. A 30-instruction program compiles in about 150 ms instead
of 1.4 s.

With RECOMP_SHADER_CACHE naming a directory, every compiled shader
(variants and the renderer's own) is kept there, keyed by a hash of its
source, entry, target, defines and the build time, and later runs load it
instead of compiling.

tests/nv2a_vsh_gpu runs every case through the variants too (spec), adds
min/max over an input and log, and checks the cache fills and loads.
